Speed — How Fast Does This Work?
Under 100 milliseconds from signal received to decision made.
To put that in perspective:

A human blinks in 150-400 milliseconds
AegisDrone makes its threat assessment before you can blink

A DJI Phantom flying at 60 km/h covers 1.6 metres in 100 milliseconds. Our system detects it when it is still hundreds of metres away — giving operators time to respond.
The system also has a fast-track lane: if an emitter is already in the trusted database, it skips all five AI models and responds in under 5 milliseconds. Like a pre-approved visitor who walks straight through without security check.

What Happens If the System Fails?
Designed for Failure — Gracefully
Scenario 1: AI models are uncertain
→ System returns "HOLD ⏸️" — does not act, alerts human operator, keeps gathering data
→ Better to be uncertain than confidently wrong
Scenario 2: A signal looks physically impossible (corrupted data, sensor malfunction)
→ System immediately flags it as "OPEN_SET_UNKNOWN ❓" and alerts operator
→ Does not make a false threat call
Scenario 3: Power failure / connectivity loss
→ System runs entirely on local hardware. No cloud dependency. Continues operating.
Scenario 4: Adversarial drone mimicking a friendly signal
→ The Hysteresis Filter requires the threat classification to appear consistently across 5 consecutive readings before triggering jamming. A one-time spoof doesn't activate countermeasures.
→ The DeepSVDD anomaly detector looks at the physics of the signal — mimicking the label is possible, mimicking the exact RF physics is extremely difficult.
Scenario 5: Multiple drones simultaneously
→ Each drone gets its own emitter ID (radio fingerprint hash). Tracked independently and simultaneously.
→ Acknowledged limitation: if 10 drones appear at exactly the same moment in the same frequency band, the mixed signal may confuse the system. Resolution: deploy multiple antennas.

